# Atlas documentation

## Ownership (mandatory)

- Atlas product documentation belongs in `apps/atlas-docs`.
- Nexus-wide architecture, standards, and repository decisions belong in root `/docs`.
- Do **not** duplicate the same source of truth in both places. Link across boundaries instead.

## Every completed milestone (mandatory)

Update before claiming the milestone done (see `atlas-definition-of-done`):

| Record | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| **Build Manifest** | Status, scope, checklist, next milestone — keep authoritative |
| **Implementation report** | What shipped, intentional deltas, test results |
| **Architecture report** | When boundaries, ownership, freeze scope, or abstractions change |
| **Deferred technical debt** | Explicit backlog entries — never silent drops |
| **Decision log** | Material trade-offs and approved exceptions |

Explain **why** decisions were made. Do not merely restate code.

## Atlas docs topics (as they become real)

Product overview, design principles, visual system, typography, color, spacing, motion, components, responsive behavior, page walkthroughs, Strapi content model (Atlas perspective), editorial workflows, testing, accessibility, architecture, deployment overview, maintenance, and ADRs.

## Screenshots (mandatory)

- Include desktop, tablet, and mobile screenshots for important pages.
- Generate with Playwright where practical.
- Store in a predictable Atlas docs asset directory.
- Screenshots must stay synchronized with the implementation.
- Broken image references and broken documentation links should fail validation.

## Writing standards (mandatory)

- Do not document self-explanatory functions or trivial components.
- Do not generate filler documentation.
- Keep public docs free of secrets, credentials, private endpoints, internal tokens, and sensitive operational details. Separate public technical docs from private operational notes when needed.
- Update docs when architecture, CMS workflows, major components, or testing behavior materially changes.
